    "created_at": "2022-04-25T12:53:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-04-25T12:53:40Z",
    "body": "I looked at non-`img` element callers and they're all in the media element section.\r\n\r\n* https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-media-load-algorithm The resource selection algorithm for a media element. This seems to violate the contract and call the algorithm from something that is not running in parallel. (If it is running in parallel, it could not update element state as it appears to be doing. So either way it's bogus.)\r\n* Further down in the same algorithm it is used correctly twice (I think), but it's unclear why it doesn't queue a media element task. In particular, in these the synchronous sections it actively mutates element state. If you're in parallel, that's a thing you need to do from a task. (Again, I don't think there's a use case for ever queuing a microtask from in parallel without a wrapping task, but I hope we can at least agree that mutating observable state in such microtasks makes it extra bad.)\r\n* \"Any time the user agent [provides a stable state](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#await-a-stable-state), the [official playback position](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#official-playback-position) must be set to the current playback position.\" I'm not sure what to make of this. Perhaps this should happen as part of the rendering loop?\r\n* The next instance happens as part \"When a [media element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#media-element) is removed from a Document, the user agent must run the following steps:\" This seems completely bogus. Element removal never happens in parallel. Should be easy to remove.\r\n* https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-media-seek Seeking does it at the end of the algorithm, mutating element state. Again, unclear why no media element task is used. Apart from mutating state, this also ends up queuing a lot of additional tasks, a lot of which could happen synchronously in the same task and might well happen synchronously in implementations? Do `timeupdate` and `seeked` events really allow for interleaving tasks and microtasks?\r\n* Last instance is in \"start the track processing model\" but it's run immediately after going in parallel. Why not run that first and then go in parallel? Again, it seems to be mutating state which is weird.\r\n\r\nMost of these seem ripe for removal and some even seem rather straightforward. Am I missing something here or should I start removing some of the easier ones?\r\n\r\ncc @whatwg/media and in particular @foolip who has been involved in discussions regarding this dating back to 2014 at least 😊",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
